'\" t
.TH BOOTO 1 2026-11-09 "Booto" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
booto \- live forensic scanner for Linux rootkits and bootkits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B booto
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR dev ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-V ]
.br
.B booto
.RB [ \-d | \-m | \-u | \-v | \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B booto
inspects a suspect machine's disk from a separate, trusted live system.
It never runs code from the target: block devices are mounted read-only,
and every engine reads files and sectors as plain data.
On the live USB a udev rule also sets every disk except the Booto USB
read-only as soon as it appears
.RB ( "blockdev \-\-setro" ;
undo for one device with
.BR "blockdev \-\-setrw" ).
Reports record the SHA-256 of the target's first 1 MiB before and after the
scan, so they show the scan changed nothing there.
.PP
Without
.BR \-c ,
.B booto
starts the full-screen text interface (TUI). With
.BR \-c ,
it runs one headless scan and exits with a verdict code (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ).
.PP
A scan runs six engines on the target. Each finding they can report is
listed by ID in
.BR booto-checks (7).
.TP
.B MBR/VBR
Sector 0, the gap before the first partition, and each partition's volume
boot record: signature, partition table, known bootkit byte patterns, and
code hidden in the gap.
.TP
.B Kernel
Every kernel module under
.IR /lib/modules :
rootkit symbol signatures, missing license, and unsigned modules on a kernel
whose own modules are signed. Compressed modules are unpacked in memory.
.TP
.B UEFI
Every EFI executable on the EFI System Partition: Secure Boot DBX revocation,
Authenticode signature and image hash, SBAT revocation, packed code, and
BlackLotus-style file layout.
.TP
.B Initramfs
Each initramfs image, unpacked in memory: boot scripts that open reverse
shells, fetch code, or load modules from temporary paths.
.TP
.B Preload/persistence
.IR /etc/ld.so.preload ,
.IR /etc/environment ,
systemd units, cron, shell profiles, SSH authorized keys, modprobe and udev
rules, and known rootkit install paths.
.TP
.B Package integrity
Packaged system files are hashed and compared with the dpkg, rpm (4.16 and
later, SQLite) or pacman database of the target.
.PP
When
.B booto
scans a device (not a directory), it also audits the firmware of the machine
it is running on: Secure Boot state, keys, IOMMU, SMM and BIOS write
protection. That audit is reported separately and does not change the verdict.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the option summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show the version, git hash and build time, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cli
Run one headless scan instead of the TUI. Without
.B \-t
or
.BR \-s ,
it picks a target partition by itself and stops with
.B No scan target
when it finds none.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the planned scan (target, engines, rule files and report path) and exit
without touching any disk.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mock\-drives
Show virtual drives in the TUI. For testing the interface without real disks.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target " \fIdev\fR"
Raw block device to scan, for example
.IR /dev/sda .
Its partitions are mounted read-only under a private directory, and the EFI
System Partition is found and mounted automatically. A LUKS partition must be
unlocked first (F3 in the TUI).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-scan\-dir " \fIpath\fR"
Scan a root file system that is already mounted at
.IR path .
The MBR/VBR engine and the firmware audit are skipped.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-persistence\-dir " \fIdir\fR"
Write the case report to
.IR dir :
.I CASE\-<date>\-<time>.json
and
.IR .md ,
each with a
.I .sha256
seal file.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update\-rules
Regenerate the rule files by running
.IR scripts/update_rules.sh .
Nothing is fetched from the network. The script signs the files only when
.B BOOTO_RULES_SIGNING_KEY
names the release key. It works only from the root of the
Booto source tree; on the live USB it exits 1 and tells you to copy new rule
files to
.I /persistence/rules
instead.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-expert\-firmware
CLI mode, as root only. Also scan UEFI variables for large high-entropy blobs
(hidden code) and dump the SPI flash read-only with
.BR flashrom (8).
Slower; off by default.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quarantine
CLI mode, with
.BR \-p .
Copy each flagged file into a ZIP under
.IR dir/quarantine ,
encrypted with the password
.BR infected .
Each file is stored as
.I <path>.malware
so it cannot run by accident when the ZIP is opened elsewhere.
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline " file"
CLI mode. Also write the SHA\-256 of the boot chain to
.IR file :
the first 1 MiB of the device (MBR, VBR and the gap after the MBR), every file under
.IR /boot ,
every file on an auto\-found EFI system partition, and the Windows core boot files.
One
.RI \(dq hash "  " label \(dq
line per file, like
.BR sha256sum (1).
Run it on a machine you trust, and keep the file on the report partition.
.TP
.BI \-\-compare " file"
CLI mode. Hash the same files and list each one that is
.BR changed ,
.B missing
or
.B new
since the baseline in
.IR file .
An OS or boot loader update explains most changes; anything else is worth a look.
The list does not change the verdict or the exit code.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-verbose
Show debug messages.
.SH TUI
The TUI shows tiled panes: Storage, Scanner modules, Live logs, Threat
inspector and Case report. Click to focus a pane or select a row, click a bar
button, and use the wheel to scroll. Drag a border to resize the panes on
either side; the cell next to a border grabs it too. Over a border the pointer
turns into a left-right or up-down arrow, and the border being dragged is
drawn in bold cyan.
.PP
Right-click a pane for a menu of its actions (mount, toggle, scan, inspect,
export) and the layout ones (zoom, split, swap, resize); pick with the mouse,
or with Up, Down and Enter; Esc or a click elsewhere closes it. Double-click a
drive to mount or unlock it, or an engine to switch it on or off. A bar button
or menu row under a moving pointer is underlined.
.PP
On the Linux console, run as root,
.B booto
reads mice and VM tablets directly from
.I /dev/input/event*
and draws its own pointer, a reverse-video cell. It holds them while it runs
and hands them back to
.BR gpm (8)
in the root shell. Elsewhere (a desktop terminal, SSH) the terminal's own
mouse reporting is used.
.PP
A tab strip under the top bar switches pages:
.B 1 Panes
(the tiled panes),
.B 2 Findings
(every finding of the last scan, with the selected one's rule, hash and detail),
.B 3 Firmware
(this machine's firmware checks) and
.B 4 Evidence
(target, mount, before/after hash of the first 1 MiB, quarantine and what each
engine read). Press the digit or click the tab;
.B Esc
returns to the panes.
.SS Keys
.TP
.B E
Switch between the simple view (Storage and Threat inspector, the default) and
the expert view, which adds the Engines and Log panes and the pane layout keys
.RB ( Tab ", " Alt+Z ", " Ctrl+R ).
.TP
.BR 1 ", " 2 ", " 3 ", " 4
Show the Panes, Findings, Firmware or Evidence page.
.TP
.B F5
Scan the drive selected in Storage.
.TP
.B F6
Export the case report (JSON and Markdown, with SHA-256 seals) to report storage.
.TP
.B F7
Toggle quarantine for the next scan (see
.BR \-q ).
.TP
.B F3
Unlock the selected LUKS drive with its passphrase.
.TP
.B F4
Unlock the encrypted report partition on the Booto USB.
When the USB has none, run
.B booto\-storage create
to make one in its free space.
.TP
.BR F10 ", " q
Open the exit menu:
.B P
power off,
.B R
reboot,
.B S
root shell (type
.B exit
to return to Booto),
.B Q
quit to the console,
.B Esc
back.
.TP
.B Tab
Focus the next pane.
.TP
.BR Alt+arrow
Focus the pane in that direction.
.TP
.B Alt+Z
Zoom the focused pane to full screen, or back.
.TP
.B Alt+M
Swap the focused pane with the next one.
.TP
.BR Alt+H ", " Alt+V
Split the focused pane horizontally or vertically.
.TP
.BR Alt+[ ", " Alt+]
Shrink or grow the focused split by 5%.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Resize mode:
.BR h / j / k / l
or arrows move the focused border by one cell,
.BR H / J / K / L
by five;
.BR Esc ,
.B Enter
or
.B Ctrl+R
leave it.
.TP
.BR Up / Down ", " j / k
Move the selection or scroll a line.
.BR PgUp / PgDn ,
.BR Home / End
scroll a page or to the ends.
.TP
.B Enter
In the Threat inspector, open the Inspect view of the first flagged file.
.SS Inspect view
A read-only view of the flagged file's first bytes. The
.B Map
tab lists its hashes and its sections with offset, size and whether they hold
code; the
.B Hex
tab shows the bytes.
.BR 1 / 2
or
.B Tab
switch tabs,
.BR j / k ,
.BR PgUp / PgDn
and
.BR g / G
scroll,
.B Esc
or
.B q
close it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
In CLI mode:
.TP
.B 0
CLEAN: every enabled engine ran and found nothing.
.TP
.B 1
THREATS: at least one engine reported a threat.
.TP
.B 2
INCOMPLETE: an engine failed, nothing ran, or the scan could not start.
A clean result from the other engines does not prove the target clean.
.PP
.B \-u
exits with the status of the rule generator. The other options exit 0, or 1
on an unknown option.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Messages go to standard error as
.BR INFO ,
.BR WARN
or
.BR ERROR .
An
.B ERROR
during a scan marks its engine
.B FAILED
and makes the verdict INCOMPLETE. The common ones:
.TP
.B Cannot open block device '\fIdev\fB'
The device does not exist, or
.B booto
is not running as root.
.TP
.B Cannot mount \fIdev\fB read-only
The partition has an unknown or damaged file system, is LUKS-locked, or is
mounted elsewhere read-write. Unlock it, or mount it yourself and use
.BR \-s .
.TP
.B Failed to open LUKS container '\fIdev\fB': invalid passphrase
Wrong passphrase. Nothing on the drive changes; try again.
.TP
.B Decompressor '\fIname\fB' not available
A module or initramfs is compressed with a format whose tool is missing. That
part was not inspected. The live ISO ships gzip, xz, zstd, lz4 and bzip2.
.TP
.B Berkeley DB rpm database (rpm < 4.16) is not supported
The target's rpm database is too old to read; package integrity was not
verified.
.TP
.B Rules signature invalid for '\fIfile\fB'
.PD 0
.TP
.B Rules signature '\fIfile\fB.sig' missing or malformed
.PD
A rule file has no
.I .sig
file, or its Ed25519 signature does not verify against the public key built into
.BR booto .
The file was changed or not signed by the Booto release key. The engine that needs it fails closed instead of scanning with altered
rules. Restore the file or run
.BR "booto \-u" .
.TP
.B Rootkit file indicators unavailable
The IOC rule file could not be loaded; known install paths were not checked.
.TP
.B Firmware: this chipset is not confirmed yet
Some firmware checks read chipset registers whose layout is only verified on
listed chipsets; those checks are reported as unknown.
.TP
.B Out of memory: quarantine export disabled
The scan continues without copying files to the quarantine ZIP.
.PP
Internal error codes, shown as
.BR "err: \fIn\fR" :
.TS
l l.
0	OK
\-1	generic failure
\-2	invalid argument
\-3	out of memory
\-4	read or write error
\-5	mount failed
\-6	permission denied (run as root)
\-7	not found
\-8	corrupt or malformed data
\-9	LUKS volume is locked
\-10	cancelled
.TE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BOOTO_RULES_DIR
Look for rule files in this directory first.
.TP
.B BOOTO_RULES_DIR_ONLY
When set to
.BR 1 ,
look only in
.BR BOOTO_RULES_DIR .
.TP
.B BOOTO_ASCII
When set to
.BR 1 ,
draw the banner with
.B #
instead of block characters.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, print no colors.
.TP
.B USER
Examiner name written into case reports.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/booto/rules/
Signed rule files:
.IR lkm_rootkit_signatures.json ,
.IR mbr_bootkit_signatures.json ,
.IR rootkit_file_iocs.json ,
.IR uefi_dbx_revocations.json ,
each with a
.I .sig
Ed25519 signature. Searched after
.B BOOTO_RULES_DIR
and
.IR /persistence/rules ,
before
.IR ./rules .
Built-in copies are used when no file is found.
.TP
.I /persistence/
Report partition on the Booto USB (label
.BR BOOTO_DATA ,
optionally LUKS-encrypted). It holds
.I cases/
(case reports and quarantine ZIPs),
.I logs/
and
.IR rules/ .
A signed rule pack copied into
.I rules/
replaces the ISO's rules at the next start; an unsigned or altered file there is refused.
Without it, reports stay in RAM and are lost at power off.
On a USB written raw from the ISO (dd, Rufus DD mode, caligula),
.B booto\-storage create
(or
.B F4
in the TUI) adds it in the free space after the live system, asking for size and passphrase first;
.B booto\-storage delete
erases it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan a disk and save the report on the USB:
.PP
.RS
.B booto \-c \-t /dev/nvme0n1 \-p /persistence/cases
.RE
.PP
Same, also quarantining flagged files:
.PP
.RS
.B booto \-c \-t /dev/sda \-p /persistence/cases \-q
.RE
.PP
Scan a root file system you already mounted:
.PP
.RS
.B booto \-c \-s /mnt/suspect
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR booto-checks (7),
.BR cryptsetup (8),
.BR flashrom (8)
